WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.922
Morning, Ziad.

00:00:00.922 --> 00:00:02.107
Monday the second.

00:00:02.107 --> 00:00:05.530
Pretty simple one today, with the evening wide open.

00:00:05.530 --> 00:00:08.163
Something to think about while you move.

00:00:08.163 --> 00:00:12.837
When a loudness meter averages a whole episode, what does it leave out?

00:00:12.837 --> 00:00:14.153
Answer's at the end.

00:00:14.153 --> 00:00:15.141
Weather's easy.

00:00:15.141 --> 00:00:22.448
Dry all day, cool this morning and up into the mid-sixties by afternoon, with only a light breeze for the ride.

00:00:22.448 --> 00:00:23.370
Okay, the day.

00:00:23.370 --> 00:00:27.254
Calendar has gym at seven, then Godinger from nine to five.

00:00:27.254 --> 00:00:29.097
Nothing on there after that.

00:00:29.097 --> 00:00:35.351
It's the first workday since the clocks went back, so it'll already be dark when you head home.

00:00:35.351 --> 00:00:39.498
Worth making sure the bike lights are charged before you leave.

00:00:39.498 --> 00:00:40.420
Alright, news.

00:00:40.420 --> 00:00:45.028
You heard about the marathon going through the neighborhood yesterday.

00:00:45.028 --> 00:00:46.344
Here's how it ended.

00:00:46.344 --> 00:00:57.601
A Swedish runner named Andreas Almgren won the men's race in his very first marathon, just over two hours and six minutes, and only thirteen seconds ahead of second place.

00:00:57.601 --> 00:01:01.288
Nobody from Sweden had ever won a major marathon before.

00:01:01.288 --> 00:01:07.476
On the women's side, Sheila Chepkirui from Kenya won her second New York title in three years.

00:01:07.476 --> 00:01:09.780
The best part was right behind her.

00:01:09.780 --> 00:01:15.902
An American, Jess McClain, grabbed second by less than half a second in the last few strides.

00:01:15.902 --> 00:01:20.313
That's the first American on the podium here since twenty eighteen.

00:01:20.313 --> 00:01:21.893
Tomorrow's Election Day.

00:01:21.893 --> 00:01:30.121
Polls are open from six in the morning to nine at night, and early voting's over, so tomorrow's the day if you haven't voted.

00:01:30.121 --> 00:01:37.560
The top of the ticket is governor, with Kathy Hochul running against Bruce Blakeman, the Nassau County executive.

00:01:37.560 --> 00:01:40.852
Attorney general and comptroller are on there too.

00:01:40.852 --> 00:01:47.303
And there are five ballot proposals too, which are easy to skip past if you're not ready for them.

00:01:47.303 --> 00:01:48.488
And one where A.I.

00:01:48.488 --> 00:01:49.475
meets New York.

00:01:49.475 --> 00:01:52.504
Last week the governor signed a law about A.I.

00:01:52.504 --> 00:01:53.688
chatbots and kids.

00:01:53.688 --> 00:02:04.748
Once it kicks in, companies can't offer chatbots to anyone under eighteen if they pretend to be human, push kids toward an emotional attachment, or encourage self-harm.

00:02:04.748 --> 00:02:09.356
The attorney general can fine companies and make them change the bots.

00:02:09.356 --> 00:02:14.030
Supporters are calling it the strongest law of its kind in the country.

00:02:14.030 --> 00:02:19.231
Tech companies aren't thrilled, so expect some pushback before it takes effect.

00:02:19.231 --> 00:02:20.810
Here's one for Thursday.

00:02:20.810 --> 00:02:27.854
Poetica Coffee on Lorimer Street in Williamsburg runs a thing called Cozy Collage Club, from seven to nine.

00:02:27.854 --> 00:02:32.857
It's fifteen bucks,, and they supply all the materials, so you just show up.

00:02:32.857 --> 00:02:35.161
It's a nice change from the screen.

00:02:35.161 --> 00:02:43.456
You spend all day making images with a camera, and collage is making images with your hands, from pictures somebody else took.

00:02:43.456 --> 00:02:46.550
It's a good way to see composition differently.

00:02:46.550 --> 00:02:51.224
And it's a coffee shop, so the vibe stays low-key, not a big night out.

00:02:51.224 --> 00:02:53.528
Today's challenge is for your body.

00:02:53.528 --> 00:02:57.149
Tonight, before bed, get on the floor for five minutes.

00:02:57.149 --> 00:03:03.205
Do one slow hip stretch on each side and one calf stretch on each side, about a minute each.

00:03:03.205 --> 00:03:05.970
No phone, just breathe and let it sink in.

00:03:05.970 --> 00:03:11.236
Between the gym, the run, and the bike, those are the parts doing the most work.

00:03:11.236 --> 00:03:13.145
Five quiet minutes is plenty.

00:03:13.145 --> 00:03:15.581
Okay, something you've been learning.

00:03:15.581 --> 00:03:20.518
Remember wiring up the memo so a push makes the audio and updates the feed?

00:03:20.518 --> 00:03:25.258
You know a fresh computer spins up, does the work, and gets thrown away.

00:03:25.258 --> 00:03:28.484
Here's the part that's a little strange about it.

00:03:28.484 --> 00:03:34.606
The repo is public, but that computer still needs a private key to talk to the voice service.

00:03:34.606 --> 00:03:37.897
So how does it get one without everyone seeing it?

00:03:37.897 --> 00:03:40.860
The answer is something GitHub calls secrets.

00:03:40.860 --> 00:03:44.020
You paste the key into the repo's settings once.

00:03:44.020 --> 00:03:49.681
GitHub stores it encrypted, and after that, nobody can read it back out, not even you.

00:03:49.681 --> 00:03:51.261
You can only replace it.

00:03:51.261 --> 00:03:56.922
When a run starts, GitHub hands the key to that throwaway computer, just for that job.

00:03:56.922 --> 00:04:01.925
The steps use it, and when the computer's thrown away, the key goes with it.

00:04:01.925 --> 00:04:03.769
There's a second layer, too.

00:04:03.769 --> 00:04:07.982
The logs from each run are public, so GitHub watches the output.

00:04:07.982 --> 00:04:14.367
If the key ever shows up in a log line, it gets swapped for a row of stars before anyone sees it.

00:04:14.367 --> 00:04:18.383
And here's the guardrail that matters most for a public repo.

00:04:18.383 --> 00:04:25.756
If a stranger copies your repo and sends you a change, the run that tests their change doesn't get your secrets.

00:04:25.756 --> 00:04:30.693
Otherwise anyone could write a step that just mails your key to themselves.

00:04:30.693 --> 00:04:33.721
The weak spot is the steps you write yourself.

00:04:33.721 --> 00:04:38.395
If a step sends the key somewhere it shouldn't, GitHub can't stop that.

00:04:38.395 --> 00:04:40.238
The safe stuff is automatic.

00:04:40.238 --> 00:04:43.530
Being careful with your own steps is still on you.

00:04:43.530 --> 00:04:48.072
So: calendar says gym at seven, Godinger till five, and a free night.

00:04:48.072 --> 00:04:50.837
Charge the bike lights, and vote tomorrow.

00:04:50.837 --> 00:04:52.680
The answer: the quiet parts.

00:04:52.680 --> 00:04:57.354
Pauses and near-silence get skipped so they don't drag the number down.

00:04:57.354 --> 00:04:59.000
Have a good Monday, Ziad.
